Trigger `ta-anchor-dynamic-refresh__finding70-experiment-scored-in-review-doc.md` (`.WIP`).
Finding 70 qualifies two things in my last handoff. Both are executable, so I ran them rather than
concede or argue on a reading. **Review is right on both, and one of the two is stronger than it
claimed.**

## 1. §2's route — CONFIRMED by execution, and it is unconditional, not data-dependent

Accepting the correction first: **my realism mechanism was wrong.** I justified `deadRC > 0` as *"an
analyzer entry that has just gone stale carries its last computed values."* There is no such
carry-forward. Verified at `saturation/engine_v2.go:235-248`: the loop refreshes
`perAnalyzer[nr.Name]` from *this cycle's* `AnalyzedAt` and then sets `nr.Live` **in place**; `Live`
is derived from history while `Result` is this cycle's output, and nothing copies a previous
`RoleCapacities` forward. So review's contrapositive holds — **in production, `Live == false` implies
this cycle's result is non-informative** — and the only route to `deadRC > 0` is a non-informative
result that nonetheless carries positive per-role `RequiredCapacity`.

That route exists. Called `applyUniversalThreshold` directly on the state review describes — every
variant `Reason: ReasonNoData` (so non-informative), role demand positive, no anticipated supply —
with the shipped watermarks (0.85 / 0.70):

| | value |
|---|---|
| `ResultIsInformative` before / after | **false / false** |
| model `RequiredCapacity` | 941.176 |
| role `prefill` `RequiredCapacity` | **470.588** |
| role `decode` `RequiredCapacity` | **470.588** |

**Stronger than "keyed on different fields":** `applyUniversalThreshold`
(`saturation/engine_v2.go:476-513`) computes `rc.TotalDemand/scaleUp - rc.TotalAnticipatedSupply` for
every role and **cannot see `Reason` at all** — there is no reference to `VariantCapacities` anywhere
in the function. The two properties are orthogonal *by construction*, so the route is not a
data-dependent coincidence: any non-informative result with positive role demand carries positive
per-role RC, always.

**One refinement that matters for the fixture, and it goes against my own instrument.** In the real
route **every** role's RC is positive (both read 470.588 above), because the post-step applies
uniformly. My dispatch fixture seeded **decode only**. The conclusion is unchanged — at base any one
positive value diverts to scale-up; at HEAD the entry is pruned so none of it is read — but my
fixture was a *weaker* form of the production state than I presented it as. If the Type 1 or a
regression test describes this state, it should say all roles, not one.

Both of §2's consequences accepted without reservation: the regime is production-reachable so the
HEAD-only attribution stands; and **a stale-timestamp test will not reproduce it** — it lands in
`deadRC = 0`, the inherited regime. Only a hand-built ballot reaches it, so my fixture carries no
information about frequency, and frequency is still open.

## 2. §3's inversion — accepted, and I am not running a second mutation to "confirm" it

Review's point: the natural repair — hand the guard the model's variant set and restore #1237's
intent — **would enable prefill → 0 and make `AD5` strictly worse**, so the accident must be
preserved deliberately or replaced, not tidied up. Correct, and **already established by the
mutation I ran**: both routes (disabling the clause, or handing it a set where the prefill variant is
no longer last-with-nothing-above-it) suppress it for prefill, and the disable-mutation measured 0 on
both paths at every height. A model-level-set mutation would additionally let prefill's gate shed
*decode* replicas, confounding the number — it is the worse instrument for the same claim, so I
declined to run it rather than produce a number that cannot be read cleanly.

Also agreed and worth keeping: the rule's own comment at `:157-158` is already *set-relative*, so the
defect is in the **set handed to it**, not in the rule.

## 3. What §3's alternative would actually cost — measured, and it is less than review implies

Review's other branch is *"replaced by an explicit per-role floor."* **That mechanism already exists
and needs no new code.** `scaleDownVariantSet` reads a per-variant `MinReplicas` floor at
`cost_aware_optimizer.go:143-149` — **before** the cheapest-at-1 clause, so it dominates it. Measured
on the AD5 fixture, with the clause both live and disabled:

| | `MinReplicas` unset | `MinReplicas = 1` | `MinReplicas = 2` |
|---|---|---|---|
| clause live (today) | pf=**1** | pf=**1** | pf=**2** |
| clause disabled (post-"tidy up") | pf=**0** | pf=**1** | pf=**2** |

n ∈ {2,4,8}, identical at every height. So the explicit floor **fully substitutes** for the accident
and is strictly more expressive — a floor of 2 is reachable, which the accident cannot give.

**The correction to draw, which the measurement makes rather than softens:** `MinReplicas` is a
per-variant field set by the operator, not something the optimizer synthesizes. So "replaced by an
explicit per-role floor" resolves into two different answers with different costs — (a) tell
operators to set `MinReplicas` on prefill variants, which works today but is documentation, not a
fix, and leaves every unconfigured P/D model exposed; or (b) have the code synthesize a floor for a
role whose demand nobody priced, which is new behavior and a Type-1 question. My run establishes that
(a) works and that (b) would need no new plumbing — the read is already in the right place. Which of
the two, or neither, is the planner's and Dean's.

